{{smaller block|That ''aural'' acquaintance with Latin phrases which the unlearned might pick up from pulpit quotations constantly interpreted by the preacher, could help them little when they saw written Latin. {{right|''George Eliot'', Romola, lxiii.}}}}

'''auraminē''' (â′ra-min), ''n.'' [< ''aurum'' + ''amine.''] A coal-tar color used in dyeing. {{smaller|It is the hydrochlorid of tetra-methyl-diamido-benzo-phenon-imide. It yields a pure and brilliant yellow on cotton, wool, and silk.}}

'''aurantia''' (â-ran′shi-ä), ''n.'' [NL., < ''aurantium'', an orange: see ''orange.''] A coal-tar color used in dyeing. {{smaller|It is the ammonium salt of hexa-nitro-diphenylamine. It produces shades of orange, but is only applied to wool and silk. It has been said that this dye has poisonous properties, occasioning skin-eruptions.}}

'''Aurantiaceæ''' (â-ran-ti-ā′sē-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''aurantium'', an orange (see ''orange''), + ''-aceæ.''] See ''Aurantieæ.''

'''aurantiaceous''' (â-ran-ti-ā′shius), ''a.'' [< NL. ''aurantiaceus'': see above.] Of or belonging to the ''Aurantiaceæ.''

'''Aurantieæ''' (â-ran-tī′ẹ̄-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''aurantium'' (see ''orange'') + ''-eæ.''] A tribe in the natural order ''Rutaceæ'', trees or shrubs, distinguished from the rest of the order by their perfect flowers and by their fruit, a large berry with exalbuminous seeds. {{smaller|It has often been classed as a distinct order, the ''Aurantiaceæ.'' There are about a dozen genera, indigenous to tropical Asia, of which the most familiar are ''Citrus'', yielding the orange, etc.; ''Limonia'' (which see); and ''Ægle'', the bhel-tree.}}

